  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 309 AM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Maintained yesterday's forecast change of\nincluding some showers and very isolated convection today. Best\nchances of thunderstorms lie in the St Lawrence Valley where the\nCAPE gradient will be sharpest with around 500J/kg to work with.\nOtherwise, temperatures will be similar to yesterday with highs\nin the mid/upper 70s to low 80s today and Thursday. Shower and \nthunderstorm chances increase Thursday as low pressure moving \nthrough southern Canada pushes a boundary through the region.\nAround 500 J/kg of CAPE is projected to be in the region\ncoincident with the weakening boundary with the strongest\nforcing towards the Canadian border(CAMs are highlighting \npotential for a bullseye of 1000+J/kg at max heating which may \nstrafe our northern zones in Vermont - lower likelihood, but \npotentially impactful scenario where a couple stronger storms \nwould be possible). Another trough is anticipated to move \nthrough Friday in broad northwest flow. Showers are mostly \nexpected, but could see a few rumbles in southern Vermont where \ntiming will play a roll in convective chances. Should the trough\nslow, surface instability will likely build enough for more \nshowers and storms, but current timing of this wave supports \nmainly showers as the wave exists early afternoon. Some breezy\nconditions should be anticipated with this trough, especially on\nLake Champlain and the peaks where gusts could approach or \nexceed 30 mph. Gusts 15 to 25 mph are probable at lower\nelevations away from Lake Champlain. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A mid-level ridge over the southeastern US will \nbuild northward a bit on Saturday, leaving us in northwesterly \nflow with dry air and no precipitation chances. This ridge will \nbreakdown on Sunday, allowing a weak wave of low pressure \noriginating in the foothills of the Colorado Rockies to zip \neastward and bring us some overrunning/warm frontal type of \nrain. There is a slight chance of thunder indicated at this time\nSunday night in much of Vermont, which would only be a factor \nif the low pressure system trended stronger and/or tracked \nfarther north, such that our region got into the system's warm \nsector. Otherwise, a steady soaking rain looks favored, with \ncurrent consensus timing from Sunday afternoon through Monday \nmorning. Probabilities of event total rainfall exceeding 0.5\" \ncurrently ranges from about 20% to 40%, tapering off generally \ntowards the north. among the latest global ensemble scenarios, \nsome solutions are a little slower to bring rain to an end on \nMonday with higher probabilities of rain into Monday evening \nthan what the current forecast suggests. Given the \nuncertainties, the 10-30% chance of showers Monday afternoon \nlooks reasonable at this time; some of this precipitation could \nbe orographic, post-frontal showers. \n\nFollowing this system it appears a ridge of high pressure may build \nin, which would provide us with generally quiet weather for Tuesday. \nThere are weak signals for perhaps a terrain-driven shower in the \nAdirondacks or off of a lake breeze interface, which would make \nsense given a typical summertime heating scenario with lack of other \nforcing mechanisms for precipitation.",
